{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure node1 to operate as the internal time source for the cluster.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 runs enabled chronyd, permits the cluster subnet, listens on UDP/123, and retains the required firewall and server configuration after restart. Node2's successful selection of 10.100.36.10 provides direct end-to-end evidence that node1 serves time.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a2b0533d49fe4346","cmd-6385f44f242e406f","cmd-3675a530bcc24aaf","cmd-0358a123a5c74636","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Configure node2 to synchronize against node1 instead of a public time server.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2's effective configuration contains only server 10.100.36.10, with no public pool or DHCP source. Chronyd selected that address, waitsync succeeded, and NTPSynchronized=yes both before and after node2's restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-69c1307fb8ef4c6e","cmd-cb5712ebff324c67","cmd-cbc02bf82c48475b","cmd-0358a123a5c74636","cmd-ed1f49f5ed6245c7"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the existing platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on CentOS Stream 10 with kernel 6.12.0-205.el10.x86_64. The existing chrony 4.8 package was used, and final package-health observations report no inconsistency.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b61be1efecdd442a","cmd-31150c2e227c4044","cmd-3c9d0d6a906948f0","cmd-ed1f49f5ed6245c7","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":1.0,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation was identified. The chrony configuration, firewall change, service restart/enable operations, and rollback backups directly supported the task; the scheduled node restarts supported the mandatory restart protocol. Transient failed connection attempts occurred during the expected reboot windows and show no unwanted mutation. Both nodes finished with no failed services, healthy package state, and only expected reboot-generated temporary files. Restart evidence is complete for both nodes: healthy pre-restart state, accepted restart scheduling, changed boot identities, recovered services, persisted configuration, and recovered cross-node synchronization; fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a2b0533d49fe4346","cmd-69c1307fb8ef4c6e","cmd-22f755cc8c874f1d","cmd-b5572ed9be984e81","cmd-3675a530bcc24aaf","cmd-dfb609d34f8d4111","cmd-57f3c9445e24428e","cmd-0358a123a5c74636","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. Node1 demonstrably serves NTP to the cluster, node2 uses and selects node1 exclusively, synchronization persists across sequential restarts, and the original OS release and kernel remain intact. Provider-wide restart evidence is complete and consistent with the lifecycle snapshots.","limitations":[]}
